That's a *really* dicey standard.  There are *tons* of things, all
within the "users' level of privilege," that can reduce a system to
slag.  Quite frankly, most of these things *cannot* be stopped with
mere privileges.  Consider that the command:
	while true
	do
	mkdir foo
	cd foo
	done
will toast any disk partition to which the user has write access, (in
the absence of inode quotas).  I don't think that any of us would find
the intentional execution of such a command acceptable, but we cannot
remove this from the "users' level of privilege."

To make the obligatory "real world analogy," it's within your "level of
privilege" to pick up a piece of wood and bludgeon me...but that doesn't
make it legal, and I can't really remove it from your "level of privilege."

