From caf-talk Caf Feb  7 21:46:33 1994
From: iwj@cam-orl.co.uk (Ian Jackson)
Subject:  Proposal: alt.net.abuse.petitions moderated
Message-ID: <1994Feb7.115317.28699@infodev.cam.ac.uk>
Date: Mon, 7 Feb 1994 11:53:17 GMT

There has been a lot of growth recently in various kinds of junk
email, mostly sent by people who trawl news postings for addresses to
mail to.

Unfortunately many of these sites are insensitive to conventional
avenues of complaint, since the admins often support the actions of
the errant users, and the service providers cannot or will not
intervene, usually for legal reasons.  The perpetrators usually don't
care how many people they piss off, because even very low positive
return rates are considered successful.

The only way to get through to these people is to make what they are
doing a problem *for them*, rather than for the rest of us.

I therefore propose a newsgroup (which I tentatively suggest should be
named alt.net.abuse.petitions) in which people can post a request that
a particular site's administration be informed that what they have
done is considered opprobrious.

Readers could then choose for themselves whether the incident in
question was serious, and reply accordingly.

I propose that the group be moderated by a daemon which will ensure
that every poster to the group gets a copy of the submission
guidelines the first time they post, and then post their request when
they resubmit it.  This might be achieved by requiring posters to
place a particular string in the Summary header, for example.

I propose the following submission guidelines:

 When first receiving a piece of unsoliticted junk mail, you should
 make sure that the message did not arrive as part of a subscription
 to a mailing list which you asked to be subscribed to and on which
 that kind of material is considered acceptable, and that you didn't
 ask for it to be sent to you.

 You should then make a complaint to Postmaster at the relevant site.

 If you have not received a satisfactory response within three
 business days (bear timezones in mind!) you may decide to post to
 alt.net.abuse.petitions.

 An apology from the site's admin and an assurance that they will
 attempt to prevent it happening again should be considered a
 satisfactory response, unless you have evidence that they are
 apologising in bad faith and continuing their activities regardless.

 Your posting should contain copies of all the email you have sent and
 received on the subject, including the original offending message,
 and any other information you have available or comments you wish to
 add.  It is appropriate in this situation to relax the usual rule
 that personal email should not be posted.

 Your posting should state at the top the net addresses you wish
 messages supporting your complaint to be sent to.

 Note that posting false information to alt.net.abuse.petitions could
 involve you in all sorts of legal problems ...

 You should not post complaints about news postings to this group.

 Postings to alt.net.abuse.petitions should contain a Followup-To
