	I am a student at Providence College and I am interested in the
recent attempt to initiate a Black Studies program.  This idea has met
with much opposition especially with Administration members (although
not all members). Whether racially motivated or not, I was wondering
if this issue is one of academic freedom.  While a large percent of
the population of this homogeneous, white, Roman Catholic student body
remains indifferent to the struggle, many feel it is essential to en-
couraging diversity of population and thought, to ease relations between
races and ethnicities, and to combatting a national problem.  Below is
a copy of the Mission Statement of the proposed program.  Is this an
academic freedom issue and if so can anyone offer suggestions?
 
	Providence College Black Studies Program
		Purpose and Practice
 
	The purpose of the Providence College Black Studies program is to
contribute to the end of endemic racism in the U.S.  The systematic study
is the mechanism though which this is to be accomplished.  Racism is not
an accidental characteristic of American Society; its existance and
maintenance are consequences of the political and economic orthodoxy.
Driven and directed by a worldview that stresses a functional relationship
between theory and practice, the elimination of racism requires concerted
and continuous political and economic counter action.
	The Black Studies Program is an instrument of Black people who
happen to be black students.  It supports all those courses, practices,
and projects that directly and systematically challenges America's core
values and beliefs.  It questions the underlying paradigm that decides the
definition of these fundamental ideas in everyday life.  Operating 
within the context of a predominantly white college, black
students bring with them the traditions of the black community
Today, these traditions are reflected in the survival struggles
of the black poor and working classes of America's urban
ghettos.  Therefore, the goals, objectives, and style of action
adopted by the Black Studies Program make discourse between
the black and white college communities on the meaning of 
American life established practice.  It is hoped that this open
and honest approach to the problem of racism, and its concomit-
ant sexism, will be consciousness raising for both black and
white students.
	Throughout the Black studies program, black students and
those who support our aims wish to offer a new source of energy
and vitality to the educational effort at Providence College.
We are certain that our perspective and the "outrageous
hypotheses" that it produces will contribute to the enrichment
of the lives of all students and the faculty here in our
mutual "search for truth."  More generally, the Program is 
designed to be supportive of broader efforts to raise America to a 
higher level of social justice and to a version of democracy
that is truly participatory.

