                 CANADIAN ASSOCIATION OF UNIVERSITY TEACHERS
                                   RELEASE

            294 Albert Street, Suite 208, Ottawa, Ontario, K1P 6E6
                     (613) 237-6885  FAX: (613) 237-2105


FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE
FEBRUARY 8, 1994


            CAUT URGES REVIEW OF FRAMEWORK REGARDING PREVENTION OF
            HARASSMENT AND DISCRIMINATION IN ONTARIO UNIVERSITIES

(OTTAWA) - The Canadian Association of University Teachers (CAUT) is asking the
Ontario Minister of Education and Training, David Cooke, to delay the
implementation date for the proposed policy guidelines for dealing with
harassment and discrimination in Ontario universities.  Some member faculty
associations have expressed concern that the guidelines could infringe academic
freedom.  CAUT has, however, taken no position to date with respect to the
Ontario proposal.

The Academic Freedom & Tenure Committee of CAUT will undertake a thorough study
of the Ontario government's Framework document , and report to the next CAUT
Council meeting in April.  "In the meantime", said CAUT President Alan Andrews,
"we hope the minister will not insist on the March 1 deadline."

Professor Andrews added: "CAUT's policy statement is recognized as the
definitive one with respect to academic freedom throughout Canada, and we need
to be sure that the Ministry's proposals are not inconsistent with it.
Otherwise acdemics in Ontario may find that their freedom to carry on research
and teaching is impeded."

CAUT represents 29,000 members of acdemic staff in universities and colleges
across Canada.


