i don't work for a university, but i do work for a similar organization.

we have been participating in the local USENET community for a long time
(9 or 10 years i think, i've only been there for 5).

we do email exchange with a large number of local sites via uucp, and now
with our internet connection, we have a fairly large amount of traffic.

all of that traffic is being done via a 386SX (donated by one of our
connections), with memory and a 300 meg disk which i scammaged together.

we no longer do news, since we haven't got the CPU or disk for it.

also, the bank of phone lines our neighbours use to dial in on is used for
our students to access our systems remotely.

a little history.

a long time ago, we had a fairly happy community of users. we all cooperated.
and we shared.  hell, there was a time when utzoo was the main long-haul link
for email and people mailed the sysadm there a cheque to help cover the
long distance bill.

our system started cooperating in the email network, then we started doing
some news.

after news volume started to grow, we had to cut back on our expiry, then,
later started to limit our feed.  this was because the system's real
purpose in life was for online student courses.

some time later, we ported the entire system over to a more modern machine.
(the 220 2 phase power and air conditioning was getting expensive, and the
machine was getting too slow)

the new system was faster, had more memory, and more disk.

it wasn't enough, news grew, and once again we had to cut back.

then, once, we heard that a student had nearly failed an online exam because
the system was so slow.  we investigated, and sure enough, the news expire
process was totally nuking the machine.

so, i cobbled together an intel box, and set it up running news and mail.
we ditched all news from the student machine.

the intel box never had enough umph to process news, so we gave up trying.

now, you might say that a 486 is cheap, memory is cheap, disk is cheap.
(hell, i've got a total of 40 Meg of RAM and 3.5 gig of disk in my systems
at home)

why don't i just get them to buy a better box, and more disk.

because management does not view news as an essential service.
it is a nice to have.  it is largely maintained on a voluntary basis
(mostly so the sysadms will have access to it.  in our environment, students
don't have access to, or even know about, netnews)

at the beginning of this diatribe, i said i don't work for a university.

well, i think that university management probably doesn't see news as an
essential service either.  where does it say you have to have netnews.

do you know how much resources it takes to keep news running efficiently?
(if that is possible at all)

why, so people can read about karla, bitch about censorship?

freedom of expression my ass.  nothing is free.  it all costs.

would you give up a couple of workstations so you can have more news?

> Oh, BTW, arguments like "If I can afford to do this, then I'm sure ...",
> are really empty. Many students I know, have enough storage on their
> computer at home, to just get by. And how can you afford this? Is it
> through your own work, funds from "The Bank of Mom And Dad", a student
> loan, or what? Ask yourself these questions. [I care not about the answer]

even better, if the students have so much better equipment at home, why the
hell don't they get their own feeds?  you can probably get a full feed for
free from someone in town if you agree to feed it downstream to a couple
others.

