I had to bring this issue to all those who believe that freedom of
expression is vital in academic settings, and so I thought that this
would be a good place to start.

The Ontario Provincial Government is trying to arrest freedom of
expression within post-secondary institutions in Ontario.  There has
been little media coverage of the legislation that is being brought
forth in the Ontario Provincial Legislature to curtail the freedom
that both professors and students are entitled to in terms of
expressing their respective view points, and as determined by what is
read in a school setting.  

I am a fourth year student of political theory and have become simply
outraged at the legislation being aired in the Legislature by the
provincial government, the NDP.  Before I continue, here is basically
an outline of the petition that a number of students at Carleton (in
Political Studies, Philosophy, Journalism, Mass Communications) have
just initiated in efforts that we may be able to continue reading that
which we want to read, and expressing views that may not be popular,
but still should be allowed to have a voice in an academic forum.

The following is our petition, which I openly invite you all to
support, or send comments:

"The undersigned students of Carleton University wish to express our
concern with the Ontario Ministry of Education and Training document
"Framework Regarding Prevention of Harassment and Discrimination in
Ontario Universities".  We believe the implementation of this
"Framework" would severely curtail academic and personal freedom;
teaching and learning cannot continue if it is prohibited to express
ideas which might be offensive to someone.  We offer you our support
in rejecting the "Framework" as providing a climate of civility in
universities, and urge you to join us."

A number of professors at the university are also circulating the
aforementioned petition.  In Canada, we are supposed to be tolerant,
and promoters of open forums of discussion.  I appeal to you all to
support us in our efforts, we do not have the resources of government,
but we are concerned with being able to study in an environment that
is conducive to learning, instead of shutting us out of certain areas
that may be controversial.

