In article <CKz0yK.8sM@cs.uiuc.edu> kadie@hal.cs.uiuc.edu (Fwd:) writes:

>The Ontario Provincial Government is trying to arrest freedom of
>expression within post-secondary institutions in Ontario.  There has
>been little media coverage of the legislation that is being brought
>forth in the Ontario Provincial Legislature to curtail the freedom

The "Framework Regarding Prevention of Harassment and Discrimination
in Ontario Universities" is *not* legislation, it is a suggested
framework for universities to create or modify their own harassment
or discrimination policies.

It is however mandatory for the Colleges of Applied Arts and Technology
in Ontario to create or modify policies such that they are consistent
with The "Framework". Once again, this is *not* legislation but
guidelines government can enforce on the Colleges (which are
non-academic institutions) as the Colleges are legally agents of
government and not autonomous from government.  The universities are
autonomous from government.

>A number of professors at the university are also circulating the
>aforementioned petition.  In Canada, we are supposed to be tolerant,
>and promoters of open forums of discussion.  I appeal to you all to
>support us in our efforts, we do not have the resources of government,
>but we are concerned with being able to study in an environment that
>is conducive to learning, instead of shutting us out of certain areas
>that may be controversial.

The guidelines in the "Framework" are fully consistent with the existing
Ontario Human Rights Code.  All Ontario residents are already subject
to these guidelines in one way or another.  One of the purposes of the
guidelines is to give colleges and universities an opportunity to create
policies and procedures on campus, appropriate to an educational
setting, avoiding the blunter tool of a human rights tribunal hearing.

One argument put forward in the "Framework" is that such guidelines
are necessary to create an enviroment conducive to learning, by ensuring
the colleges and universities are not hostile to any one because of thier
sex, race, sexual oirentation, etc.

As I posted yesterday, the Academic Freedom and Tenure Committee of
the Canadian Association of University Teachers will be conducting
an investigation of the "Framework" to determine how much of a threat
it is to academic freedom.

