The Canadian Alliance Against Software Theft (CAAST) announced it has
executed the first-ever civil search-and-seizure operation against a
company for alleged "softlifting".  Softlifting is the unauthorized
copying of software within a company or other organization.

	CAAST obtained an order in November in the Federal Court of
Canada permitting Lotus, Microsoft and WordPerfect to conduct a
search-and-seizure against the Vancouver Island Business College Inc.
(VIBC), with approximately 50 computer terminals and three classrooms,
is a private training institution.

	"A tip was received from an informant using CAAST's 1-800
anti-piracy hotline", stated Michael Eisen in a news release.

	Eisen is a partner with Morris/Rose/Ledgett and CAAST counsel.
"We conducted an investigation and ultimately obtained an order to
search the VIBC premises for infringing material.  This is the first
time in Canada that a civil search-and-seizure order has been carried
out on the basis of softlifting, which is the most pervasive form of
software piracy", he said.

	"Softlifting occurs when illegal copies are made within an
organization for employees to use in the office or to take home, or
for disk swapping," stated James Courtney, General Manager,
Quarterdeck Office Systems Canada Inc. in the news release.

