Jim Mercer (jim@reptiles.org) wrote:

: because management does not view news as an essential service.
: it is a nice to have.  it is largely maintained on a voluntary basis
: (mostly so the sysadms will have access to it.  in our environment, students
: don't have access to, or even know about, netnews)

: at the beginning of this diatribe, i said i don't work for a university.

: well, i think that university management probably doesn't see news as an
: essential service either.  where does it say you have to have netnews.

This is the entire problem. Administration does not view netnews as an
essential service. I personally could live quiet well without it I guess,
but I read alot of mailing lists. The attitude towards the entire internet
is not good around UfT or in canada in general.

I think its a fact that at Carnegie Mellon U in PGH that first year students
must (or merely have the option?) to take a course on the internet, and
are all given net accounts. (Ever wonder why you see so many posts from
people @andrew.cmu.edu on news and in mailing lists?)

This is an extremely healthy attitude. What with the words "information
superhighway" being bashed around on tv and in the media so much. I think that
it might be important to train people in our "institutions of higher learning"
to use this information superhighway in school instead of waiting til they
get out in the business world, if they get to use it then at all. I'm wondering
who are all the people that are GOING to use this info superhighway if no
one is considering it important except the US and New Brunswick govts.

I dont think we should leave compSci and engineering geeks (like me!) to
be the "owners" and users of the net. Even so, we dont get proper training
with it either. I've learned alot in the last 6 years of being on the net,
but this could all be taught in a half course at UfT, say.

And we all wonder why there is so much flaming on the Usenet, which has made
it almost useless as a serious research tool. When people get all excited
about the usenet and ask me how come I'm not all over the newsgroups, I put
on my jaded hat and say "two years too late - I'm done with unmoderated
groups."

The usenet is NOT a right, correct, but is access to the phone system? The
"info superhighway" will probably become as essential as the phone system
or more so, and the Usenet is just part of it, and used to be an important
part (and can be again if everyone used their brains before posting).

However, when demand for the priviledge of Usenet news outstrips the
department's funding and eats into work hours for staff, thats a bit much.

However, I dont totally understand the mentality behind UfT's compsci
department. We have the disk space - and students are willing to pay for
it too - disk is less than $1/meg nowadays.

All the software is in place already and CDF (compSci disciplines facility)
already handles some 40megs of news or so a day (comp, ut, tor, ont, can
and a few others) - adding a few other newsgroups (alt, etc) WILL, yes,
eat some diskspace (which the students have already offered to pay for, its
a small cost!) but I dont think it will add any extra hours to managing
the news thats already there.

They run Cnews, as do I. When I run into a problem with Cnews its almost
always the whole news system which has a problem, or a problem which could
affect any group. Its not one particular group which causes problems more
than others.

Of course if you try to run Cnews within 0.5% of maximum disk space (like
I DO!) then you're bound to have problems. But like I said, disk space is
cheap.

So, at the cost of more disk space (200-300 megs would be enough I think
to get a full feed) and adding a few more minutes of administration per
day, they could make alot of students happy.

I spose this in itself is not a concern - but then again, why did garnet.cdf
generate more than half of the UDP packets for Dec 93 in Canada? (statistic
subject to confirmation - it was large) - netrek eats up CPU and
net bandwidth for everyone. The students maintain it to an extent, but I
am sure using that much bandwidth isnt good.

However, I think there's much more than student contentment to be
gained from providing a bigger newsfeed - it can still be used as a
research tool, and in general for information collection. I come across
a large number of interesting and suprising things of great importance
to me in some alt groups. I know that some of you might not consider
censorship worthy of any bandwidth at all, or the CLIPPER chip debate
in the US (if it goes through in the US, Canada is likely to follow
suit...) worth anything, but it is.

Denying access which would cost a small amount of overhead to the department
to allow students to educate themselves, seems rather nearsighted. I wont
say its draconian - we can argue that every second spent reading news is
wasting time for other students who have serious work, etc.

However, sometimes the cost+overhead debate isnt valid.

: would you give up a couple of workstations so you can have more news?

I give up CPU time on my box so that users can login free here and read
news...

: even better, if the students have so much better equipment at home, why the
: hell don't they get their own feeds?  you can probably get a full feed for
: free from someone in town if you agree to feed it downstream to a couple
: others.

A full feed still costs the phone line. At 14.4kbps, a 70meg+ full feed/day
is going to take up most of the day to Xfer. A system on a T1 or T2 is
going to take a whole 10 minutes to get 80 megs. 

(A small feed is much more manageable.)

Besides, everyone, just run NNTP and get news from other sites. I'll try
and remember to post directions on how to do this soon.

