In this week's issue of Macleans there is an article (pg 13) on the KH media
ban appeal.   It mentions two Red River Community College students, Neil
Hiebert and Craig Knapp, that read the Washington Post article over the air at
the college station, CMOR.  They have been 'banned for life' and "more
seriously, they could face contempt of court charges - and possibly time in
jail".  The article was written by Barbara Wickens.

What the article fails to mention is something anyone in College/University
radio would know just by reading the call letters. The CRTC assigns only calls
starting with CF, CH, CI, CJ, and CK to private broadcasters.  CB is reserved
for CBC stations, and VO is reserved by special agreement to Newfoundland 
stations.  Therefore, "CMOR" is either closed-circuit within-the-college 
or available on Cable FM.  As someone who has helped out on CSCR (UofT
Scarborough), a cable-only outfit, I know from experience that listenership i
is, at best, negligable.

It will be interesting to see if there is any followup on this.  From what I
have heard, they got the info from either here, or from that mailing list run
by that 'Abdul' guy.

