[Adapted with permission from a mailing list -cmk]

McGill's schizophrenic policy
	- paper media ...  not censored
	- electronic media ... censored

The McGill lawyer has decide it is OK to accept so-called "banned
material" in newspapers and make that information available in McGill
Libraries.  Electronic versions of similar material is banned on
campus.


First Item:	Memo to McGill Legal Advisor.
Second Item:	His response.
----------------------------

	McGill					MEMORANDUM

XXXXXXXXXXXX

TO:	Mr. Raynald Mercille
	Legal Advisor

CC:	CIM Computer Committee

DATE:	January 12, 1994

-------------------------------------------------------------------

Dear Mr. Mercille,

...

I would simply like to confirm the accuracy of my interpretation
of your two legal opinions (dated 15nov93 and 3dec93 -- enclosed).

The two memos relate to ``information'' and whether its presence
on campus is prohibited by the 77 page Kovacs ruling of July 5, 1993.

According to your December 3 memo to Ms. Groen, you note that the following
actions are specifically *not* prohibited:

   * receipt of information
   * possession of information,
   * providing access to information
   * reading information on a computer screen

In addition, you pointed out that it is simply not practically possible
to monitor all sources of information on campus to watch out for so-called
"banned material".  Because of all these reasons, you concluded that,
for example, there is no legal concern associated with the McGill Libraries
receiving and making available foreign news articles in the library.
Thus, information that it might be wrong to "publish" is nonetheless
readily accessible to any member of the McGill community and is in
fact allowed, as a matter of policy.  I have visited the library and
there are indeed several such news articles now available.
You summarized by saying, "I certainly would feel quite comfortable
defending the University against any complaint of criminal wrongdoing".

I must say that I understand and agree with the logic of this decision.

In a related decision, except instead of print media it was electronic media,
you recommended that a campus-wide "ban" on an electronic newsgroup
be maintained.  The key distinction, from my reading of your arguments,
rests on this notion of "publication".  By default, electronic news articles
are propogated to neighbouring institutions using Usenet News software.
It is this propogation that may be similar enough to publication that
was the primary concern.

Having said all this, I would simply like to confirm with you that
if we *accept* news articles onto campus and allow members of the McGill
community to access these articles, but specifically *switch off* the
normal propogation of these news articles to any other institution,
or even any other "news server", then this would eliminate the concern
over "publication".  In fact, it would establish a very strong analogy
between information in the form of print media in the library
and information in the form of electronic media in a McGill Computer.

...

Respectfully yours,

(signed)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

	McGill					MEMORANDUM

FROM:	Raynald Mercille
	Legal Advisor

XXXXX

CC:	Vice-principal F.Tavenas
	Mr. Alan Greenberg, Director, Computing Centre
	Mr. S.Robbins, Systems Administrator CIM

~Date:	February 8, 1994

SUBJECT:	Removal of a USENET "newsgroup"

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Vice-principal F.Tavenas has now authorized me to reply to your
memo of January 12, 1994.

Your reading of my two opinions to Vice-Principal Tavenas and Ms. Groen
is accurate: the primary issue is not being in possession of
banned material (reading in a paper or on a screen) but rather,
transmitting it to third parties and being open to the argument of
publication.

My first opinion to Vice-Principal F.Tavenas did not distinguish
the local campus network from sites or nodes outside McGill.  Your
query now forces me to focus on the issue of service to local
users, as opposed to users outside McGill.

If I understand correctly, the procedure to make available a
newsgroup locally is as follows.  A separate file folder is created
so that local users can be fed segregated news items on demand.  If
no separate file folder is created, the newsgroup is left in a junk
file folder: in such a case, users outside McGill can be fed the
newsgroup through the junk file, while local users cannot be fed.
This means that contrary to what you were hoping, the argument of
publication is equally strong with the procedure required to feed
local users.

By receiving a newsgroup and packaging it in a special folder for
local users, the University is actually processing, editing and
promulgating a newsgroup for local consumption, all of which is an
activity which could be construed as publication.  I accordingly
conclude that because of the technical process, the risk of raising
an argument of publication is as important with the proceeding for
local users as it is for users outside McGill.

If your suggested approach had resolved the legal risk, which I do
not believe it did, I would have raised with you some very serious
policy considerations.

First, you have yourself in the past expressed an extreme
sensitivity to what you perceived as censorship.  There is no
censorship in Vice-Principal Tavenas' decision, because the
decision was not an imposition by Vice-Principal Tavenas of his own
values on others:  rather Vice-Principal Tavenas did what had to be
done by law.  Censorship is a process of editing material to
deprive another from access to it.  Vice-Principal Tavenas'
decision deprived even the supposed censor from having materials to
edit: it was a blackout for all.  The suggestion to allow McGill to
access the banned material while preventing other institutions in
Quebec from such benefit raises a real argument of censorship by
McGill of this information which McGill can access,  This form of
institutional peeping Tomism might have had public relation
implications which should have been weighed carefully.  The issue
then would no longer be legal any more, but rather pure policy and
fairplay in relation to suister universities.  The suggestion of
McGill using its place in the USENET system to deprive other
institutions of access and yet at the same time keep access is
difficult to justify and in my opinion, would open McGill to an
argument of inter institutional censorship.

Second, a decision to favour local users to the exclusion of non
McGill users should have taken into consideration the factor of the
public's perception.  Some USENET users may take great pride in the
fact that they can do what they want:  this is a case where the lack
of self restraint combined with the pride of their mission could 
prove to be their downfall.  This is an area of fascinating freedom
yet unregulated by government.  Those who push that freedom to its
limit may be inviting state regulatory intervention.  While
legally, there is no difference between a newsgroup which refers to
banned material in its totality and a newspaper which is not
exclusively devoted to it, the public might see it otherwise.  In
short, the Gazette could have published an article tomorrow on
"McGill surreptitiously avoids the spirit of the law", and we would
be left with the cold comfort of having respected the letter of the
law.  By pulling the plug on the newsgroup globally, although he
was forced to, Vice-Principal Tavenas ironically preserved the very
freedom you now enjoy.  By contrast, an unbridled approach or a
cunning evasion could possibly hasten the elimination of that
freedom.

For all these reasons, I recommend to Vice-Principal Tavenas that
his decision be maintained.

RM:lc





