Here is issue 120 of _Measure_ published by University Centers for
Rational Alternatives.

<<  MEASURE 120, January/February 1994  >>

<< ARTICLE 1 >

In a _Wall_Street_Journal_ editorial (Review and Outlook,
12/19/93) Thomas Aquinas College was referred to as leading
the opposition to the Western Association of Colleges and
Universities' attempt to impose standards of diversity as an
essential element of academic discipline.  The essay which
follows by a member of Thomas Aquinas College is a
profound response to that imposition.

DEATH OF THE MIND:
THE ANTI-INTELLECTUALISM OF "CULTURAL DIVERSITY"
IN EDUCATION

John W. Neumayr

Preface

The theme of "cultural diversity" in the schools has taken
center stage in higher education. Even small, private colleges
with traditional programs are being pressed to adopt
multiculturalism as a basic position although
multiculturalism may have little or no relevance to the
original purposes of such colleges. I am thinking in particular
of the classical liberal education college, whose curriculum
has its roots in ancient Athens and Rome. For two thousand
years it had never occurred to this educational tradition to
see itself as narrowly Western or European. Is this relatively
new separatist self-consciousness truly intrinsic to its
nature? Or is this merely an artificial designation that has the
effect of entangling the classical tradition in the "diversity"
argument? Is classical liberal education caught up in a
quarrel not of its own making and accused of crimes it did
not commit or of shortcomings that have no bearing on it?
The following essay discusses the relation that classical
liberal education has to the "diversity" issue.

To make the focus of this essay clearer, a word should be
said about both the meanings of "cultural diversity" and
"liberal education." Both meanings admit of ambiguity and
some degree of clarification is necessary. There is a highly
politicized sense of "cultural diversity," for example, that is
intimately associated with "political correctness." This
outlandish form of thought-control in which the curricula of
colleges and universities are usurped to bring about changes
in the political and social orders is so removed from genuine
education that it scarcely deserves comment. At the close of
this piece, in a postscript, I shall remark briefly on this
enormity.  But there is also a more sober view of "cultural
diversity" that appears to be sincerely interested in
multicultural studies for the sake of learning. It assumes that
not only is our population increasingly composed of diverse
races and ethnic groups but that also the diverse ethnic
traditions involved have their own merits and are worthy of
study in their own right. The Western Association of Schools
and Colleges (WASC) has taken a leading role among the
academic associations in promoting this interpretation  of
"diversity." While acknowledging that the "diversity" policy is
a recent phenomenon, WASC maintains, nonetheless, that the
policy has its roots in the established principles that have
guided education in America in modern times. It is this more
responsible sense of "cultural diversity" that I have in mind
in this paper, and to speak to it I rely on the formulation
given in the statement of "diversity" drawn up by the Western
Association of Schools and Colleges' Commission on
Diversity.

"Liberal education" also has more than one sense. Perhaps the
dominant sense in colleges today is equivalent to the
meaning of the "humanities." The "humanities" have stood for
a body of studies that is not so much concerned with reality
as with intellectual interpretations, achievements and
assumed interests of mankind. These studies purport to take
into account not only the object to be known, but also the
subject who knows and reacts to it. Social studies and
appreciation courses fall within its limits, while mathematics
and science/1 do not -- for the latter appear to leave the
knowing subject out of the picture. Although this notion of
the "humanities" is very ancient, it came into prominence
especially in the Renaissance. The classical notion of "liberal
education," on the other hand, the notion associated with
Plato and Aristotle and the Medieval Schoolmen, is purely
concerned with things or objects themselves. Human opinion
and invention are only of concern to the extent that they lead
to an understanding of reality. Rather than to make
individual man and his thought the object of study, liberal
education in this classical sense develops the tools of
learning, called the liberal arts, and seeks a grounding in
philosophy and in the variety of disciplines that form the
foundation of all wisdom. Mathematics and science are as
much a part of this tradition as are ethics and poetics.
"Liberal education" in this second sense is the focus of this
paper.

I.      Does "Diversity" Belong to Liberal Education?

Standard 4.B.2 of the WASC documents for higher education
states that "Undergraduate studies ensure, among other
outcomes:  (a) competence in written and oral
communication; (b) quantitative skills: and (c) the habit of
critical analysis of data and argument." The Standard then
goes on to say:  "In addition to those basic abilities and habits
of mind, goals also include an appreciation of cultural
diversity."

The Commission for Colleges and Universities speaks of the
concern of higher education for "cultural diversity" as falling
squarely under its "tradition of universalism," by which it
means the traditional belief that "all knowledge is valuable."
One cannot quarrel with the goal of "an appreciation of
cultural diversity" seen in this light. The goal is laudable. It
was actually always part of traditional scholarship, with
relevant experts working in specialized departments,
institutes or museums, to do research and to organize
meaningfully knowledge about specific cultures, past and
present.

Man in his diverse languages, arts, customs, and institutions
is surely an object of knowledge which unquestionably
belongs to the university; but one still may ask whether
"diversity" pertains to that part of knowledge called liberal
education. Whatever other meanings "diversity" may have,
the meaning it bears as it has an impact on a program of
liberal education contains the ideas of particularity and
uniqueness. The studies and readings urged upon a classical
liberal arts college in the name of "diversity" are in every case
urged because they precisely reflect what belongs to each
culture as a particular, self-consistent, and, thus, diverse
entity. Therefore, the question to be asked is whether or not
the limits of liberal education extend to "diversity" in these
respects.

In raising this question, we would do well to follow the
method Socrates used in Plato's dialogue _Meno_, where,
when asked whether virtue can be taught, he replied that he
cannot begin to answer that question until he first knows
what virtue is. "How am I to know a property of something
when I don't even know what it is?" said he, and, to illustrate
his meaning, added: "Do you suppose that somebody who is
entirely ignorant of who Meno is could say whether he is
handsome and rich and well-born or the reverse?" We too
should take a good look at liberal education before we
conclude that "diversity" belongs to it or the reverse.

At first glance, this may appear unnecessary. Some take it as
perfectly clear that the tradition we associate with liberal
education is entirely European. Undeniably, it originated in
ancient Greece and Rome and, subsequently, thrived in the
rest of Europe. Insofar as it has influenced the rest of the
world, it has been spread by Europeans. Even its major texts
are frequently designated "The Great Books of the Western
World." From such observations, it is often assumed that
classical liberal education is so marked by the West that it
amounts to no more than a European version of a wisdom
that is found elsewhere in equally valid yet diverse forms.
Just as there are "Great Books" of the West, there are "Great
Books" of the East, we are told and, furthermore, as there is a
liberal learning in Europe, there is likewise liberal learning in
the non-European cultures as well. Since each of the diverse
versions brings something distinct, the serious student, it is
assumed, should seek them all for the fullest possible
attainment of liberal learning. If this be the case, it is obvious
that "diversity" essentially belongs to liberal education as the
parts belong to the whole.

But the "habit of critical analysis of data and argument"
(Standard 4.B.2 (c)) will not allow us to settle into such a
"conventional" characterization. The account is seriously
flawed and trivial in what meaning it has. If  left
unchallenged, it skews the appearances so that liberal
education cannot be seen for what it really is, and,
consequently, we would not be able to say whether it has
"diversity" as a property or not.

II.     The Perils of Diverse "Universalisms"

The word "universalism" mentioned above, invoked by the
Commission as a principle in the light of which the value of
"diversity" in higher education is to be seen, has more than
just one meaning. The Commission used it in the sense of
"the collective whole of something, regarded collectively, as
the world or the universe." When the Commission said that
all knowledge is valuable and should have its place in the
university, it meant that the university is a whole made up of
many parts, a universe or world of education composed of
the many different arts and sciences. We also used this sense
of "universalism" in education in the previous section of this
paper when we referred to the many alleged culturally
diverse forms of liberal education which might be thought to
make up the whole of liberal education.

There are other senses of "universalism" however, which, like
this sense, convey the idea of a relation of one to many. In
the sense we have been discussing just now, the "one" is the
whole and "many" are the parts. But there is another one-to-
many relation which also goes by the name of "universal,"
from which the word "universalism" is formed. This is the
sense we associate with a genus or species, as, for example,
"man" signifies one nature that is found in many individual
men, or "culture" is one sort of thing that can be found in
many diverse forms around the world.  This_sort_of
_"universal"_is_no_a_whole_that_contains_many_parts_,_but
_one_that_is_found_in_many_.

The Commission, in fact, speaks of this latter kind of
universality when it says in the best Western tradition that
there are "culturally transcendent values," to which
"educational institutions should be committed, such as:
honesty, integrity, pursuit of truth, compassion, respect for
others," and the like. Subsequently, the Commission implies
without recourse to evidence that each of these values is
some one thing which is found in _every_culture_,_i.e.,_that_in_
_their_"trans-cultural"_character,_they_are_the_same_for_all_
_cultures_in_meaning_and_in_reality_.

The policy statement on "Diversity" is itself an instance of
universality in this second sense. In effect, it is one general
argument using a logic common to all men, addressing itself
to all cultural constituencies, and drawing upon values
already recognized by all in order to convince the readers
that "diversity" should be furthered. This is not a culturally
isolated argument; it is essentially "trans-cultural."

There is even a third sense of "universal" and, consequently,
of "universalism," that deserves to be mentioned. In this third
sense, the one-to-many is not of a whole to its many parts,
nor of one nature found in many subjects, but of one cause
that extends itself to many effects, such as the sun which
heats all the objects beneath it. If we look carefully at
Standard 4.B.2. mentioned above, we can see that the
Commission implicitly notes this sense and uses it. We recall
that the Standard states that the "outcomes" to be ensured by
undergraduate studies include:  (a) competence in written
and oral communication, (b) quantitative skills, and (c) the
habit of critical analysis of data and argument. The Standard
then states that to these "basic abilities and habits of mind,
an appreciation of cultural diversity" should be added. Notice
that the first three "outcomes" are characterized as "basic
abilities" in relation to the last "outcome," which is "an
appreciation of cultural diversity." In designating the skills of
communication, calculation, and analysis as "basic abilities,"
the Standard recognizes the group as the necessary means to
further study. As "abilities," they are tools to be used in the
more advanced studies such as those of "cultural diversity,"
and as "basis" they are seen to be so fundamental that no
advanced study can take place without them. They are, in
fact, like reason itself, the universal means of the whole
spectrum of learning. Thus, the group of "basic abilities"
stands to all the other arts and sciences as one-to-many.

As we turn to look at liberal education, we should keep in
mind especially the second and third senses of
"universalism," recalling that the second sense refers to the
one nature that may be abstracted from the many and the
third refers to a set of tools which the mind must use in the
pursuit of truth.

III.    False Dichotomy

The opinion that classical liberal education is divided
exclusively from non-Western cultures because it is of
European origin and development is erroneous. The several
senses of "universalism" we have been discussing make this
clear. Taking the last sense first, the sense in which we speak
of "basic abilities" as universal tools to be used throughout
education, let us look at the classical tradition to see just how
it is related to "diversity" studies.

At the opening of his treatise _On_Parts-of_Animals_, Aristotle
speaks for the whole of liberal education when he observes:

"Every systematic science, the humblest and the
noblest alike, seems to admit of two distinct
kinds of proficiency; one of which may be
properly called scientific knowledge of the
subject, while the other is a kind of educational
acquaintance with it. For an educated man should
be able to form a fair off-hand judgment as to the
goodness or badness of the method used by a
professor in his exposition. To be educated in
fact is to be able to do this and even the man of
universal education we deem to be such in virtue
of his having this ability. It will, however, of
course be understood that we only ascribe
universal education to one who in his own
individual person is thus critical in all or nearly
all branches of knowledge, and not to one who
has a like ability merely in some special subject."

We see in Aristotle's account of the liberally educated person,
that his concern is not with specialized knowledge, but with
the breadth of disciplines that makes it possible for him to
advance into any further field of knowledge. It emerges from
Aristotle's description of the generally educated man that the
whole of his liberal education stands as the "basic abilities"
brought to perfection and thus as the tools _par_excellence_
for all further inquiry. The inherent limit and purpose of his
liberal education is to prepare him for any and all
investigations. The habit of mind he possesses stands to the
rest of knowledge as the one means to the many possible
ends. In a phrase, liberal education is the _passe-partout_, the
master key that opens all the doors of wisdom. In this sense,
liberal education is the universal that extends itself into all
inquiry, including "cultural diversity." It is distinct from
"diversity" but hardly exclusive of it.

More importantly, however, a look at liberal education in
action shows us that classical education can be neither
diverse from non-European cultures nor in itself be
European. But rather, seen in the light of the second sense of
"universalism," the one in the many, liberal education stands
to "diversity" studies as a given trans-cultural reality stands
to the diverse cultural forms in which it is found around the
world. It is occupied exclusively with what is trans-cultural. A
return to the _Meno_ makes this evident. Let us take up the
dialogue where we left off with Socrates asking Meno for a
definition of virtue. Meno answers with a reference first to
man's virtue, then a woman's, then a child's and then an old
man's to which Socrates replies:

"...Suppose I asked you what a bee is, what is its
essential nature, and you replied that bees are of
many different kinds. What would you say if I
went on to ask, and what is it in being bees that
they are many and various and different from
one another? Or would you agree that it is not in
this respect that they differ, but in something
else ... Then do the same for the virtues...they all
have a common character that makes them
virtues. This is what ought to be kept in view by
anyone who answers the question, 'what is
virtue'?"

Socrates' point would be the same had Meno tried to answer
the question by citing Greek virtue, Persian virtue, or
Egyptian virtue. The answer must necessarily transcend all
cultural differences whatsoever. That Meno was a Greek had
no bearing at all on the proper answer to the question asked.
And so with every question that liberal education as such
asks.

Thus, even though classical liberal education had its origin
and growth in Europe, it never had Europe in mind, but rather
"virtue,"  "nature," "man," "democracy," and the like, in a way
that is common to all times and places. So even in the light of
the second sense of "universalism," classical liberal education
is neither isolated from non-Western cultures nor identified
with the West. Just as it was impossible for Meno to look at
Athens and define virtue as such, it was impossible for a
truly universally educated man to be thinking of Europe as a
distinct culture while pursuing the issues that educated him.

In this sense, it is a misnomer to refer to the "Great Books" as
Western or even masculine, though most of their authors
happened to be European males. The perennial value of these
works can only be due to the fact that they asked the
fundamental and enduring questions that arise from the
common experience shared by all peoples regardless of time
or place, and they treated these issues with unusual
intelligence. In the measure that books are localized or
parochial they do not endure. The "Great Books," or at least
the greatest of the "great," belong truly to the human race.
The truly classical transcends the biases and blindnesses of
the diverse cultures, including those of the West.

It would appear to be a bias and a blindness of our time and
culture to suppose that all thinkers are culturally
conditioned. In truth, the greatest authors to an astounding
degree kept their view fixed on the essential natures of the
objects they discussed. To treat them as culturally
conditioned is not to pay attention to what they have to say.
Were we able to enter into a personal conversation with Rene
Descartes, for instance, and should we happen to discuss his
position that all knowledge begins with the judgment,
"cogito, ergo sum," how foolish we would appear to all who
heard our conversation if we should dismiss his contention
on the grounds that it is merely the product of a 17th century
Frenchman. We would be as fatuous as the wife in the
humorous Thurber tale who, after hearing her husband
demonstrate the Pythagorean theorem, dismissed it
scornfully as "merely a man's way of thinking."

By the same kind of bias and blindness, we might also, going
in the opposite direction, fatuously attempt to use texts for
purposes of cultural diversity in liberal education. Were a
Mayan text on astronomy to exist that equaled Ptolemy's in
instructive value, and should we adopt it precisely because it
originated with North American natives, would we not fail in
our purpose? Were the text of genuine instructive value as a
tool of liberal education, it would surely draw our gaze to the
heavens and not to Central America.

When we return to the original sense of "universalism" in
higher education, namely, the sense that the corpus of
learning is a whole or collection of all the parts of knowledge,
it is now apparent that classical education is not related to
the other parts of knowledge, as, say, the hand is related to
the foot in the human body. It is more akin to the life's blood
that circulates through all the members, distinct from all the
other organs, yet vitalizing each. A studied appreciation of
cultural "diversity" would naturally draw liberal education
into itself and prosper because of the association.

IV.     Can "Diversity" Do Without Classical Education?

If the "basic abilities" of analysis, communication, and
calculation are needed to give us eyes to see and to
appreciate cultural diversity, how much more would classical
education awaken us and make us aware of the meaning of
the world around us? Boswell relates how Dr. Johnson
discouraged him from publishing an account of his travels to
the Continent on the grounds that he would not really have
much to say, Boswell being supposedly slow of wit. Dr.
Johnson remarked that books of travels will be good in
proportion to what a man has previously in his mind; his
knowing what to observe; his power of contrasting one mode
of life with another. As the Spanish proverb says, "He who
would bring home the wealth of the Indies must carry the
wealth of the Indies with him," so it is in traveling; a man
must carry knowledge with him if he would bring home
knowledge.

Mere exposure to "diversity" is really no substitute for the
learning that would give the power to observe, inquire, and
analyze cultures, qualifies which  liberal education seeks to
cultivate and perfect.

It is no accident that modern anthropology, ethnology,
linguistics, etc., which are _par_excellence_ trans-cultural
disciplines, arose from the minds of Westerners -- not
because they came from the West, but because to a man they
were classically educated.

V.      Diversity is not a Property of Liberal Education

Though an appreciation of cultural diversity implies liberal
education, the reverse is not true. This is not to say that a
student of liberal education should not go beyond it to the
various specialties, including "diversity." It is to say,
however, that "diversity" is not a part of liberal education
itself. For if it were, the particularity which diversity implies
would be a barrier to universal truth. Every test and every
teacher must be from some particular place and time, but it is
never these aspects that are at issue -- it is always the trans-
cultural. This being so, liberal education should pertain to all
in the human family, provided only that they have wonder.

Cultural conditioning may be present, but it is finally a
superficial factor. Once again Socrates shows us the way. He
does not lecture to crowds, but prefers to teach through a
dialogue, one-on-one, and this for a very good reason. The
opinions of a student, however acquired, must be overcome
by questions that ultimately lead the student to see the
object at issue for himself and to speak in his own right.

Thus, since liberal education is about the universal, the one
in the many, while "diversity" is about the particulars, to urge
that a program of liberal education be shaped and controlled
by "diversity" criteria, in so far as "diversity" is diverse, is to
ask that it violate its own nature and render its mission
impossible.

VI.     The Classical College Today

The curriculum of the college where I teach falls wholly
within the classical model of liberal education. Not only are
its parts, the _trivium_, _quadrivium_, and various
philosophic studies, at the center of that tradition, but the
curriculum also draws exclusively for its texts on the original
sources of that tradition, the "Great Books." The College even
follows the pedagogy of Socrates as closely as possible.
Teaching is done by way of discussions instead of through
lectures, with small groups of students in tutorials and
seminars. All of these features represent a unity intimately
joined to the classical tradition. How should such a college
respond to the call for "diversity?"

The classical tradition of one-that-is-found-in-many has
endured numerous vast cultural changes over twenty-five
hundred years. Can it survive the latest? Despite all the
ethnic changes occurring now in our society, the efforts of
today's student of liberal education would prove as futile as
Meno's if he lost sight of the trans-cultural universal. The
classical liberal arts college would play its students false if it
turned their gaze prematurely to the particulars.

Postscript

The argument throughout this essay has been that classical
liberal education and "diversity" studies do not naturally mix.
Like oil and water they may be mixed by violent shaking but
the two components naturally separate. So natural is this
separation that one cannot consider liberal education even in
its beginning as essentially Western. The point being made,
of course, is not that "cultural diversity" studies have no
place in the university; the point is only that they do not fall
among the essential parts of classical liberal education.

Apart from its intrusiveness into liberal education, the
current practice of diversity-based curricula to confront
freshman students with a smorgasbord of uncritical courses
glorifying non-Western cultures past and present actually
hinders the stated goal of fostering "an appreciation of
cultural diversity." Cultural entities themselves are not
simply "mixable." In fact, while some cultures commit their
educational institutions to the pursuit of truth, others use
schools to indoctrinate students in the articles of their
particular, often intolerant faith.  Some cultures teach respect
for everyone, while others rate women as subservient to men.
Some advise compassion and forgiveness, while others
embrace a cutting tooth-for-tooth justice. In the past, many
cultures -- like those of the Phoenician, Aztec, and Mayan --
were extremely brutal and bloody.  Human slavery was
acceptable in places even into this century, and the deeds of
the Nazis, the practices in the Gulags, the killing fields, and
the recently reported massacres in Africa and Latin America
leave no continent free of fresh cultural question marks.
Attempts then (as most extant diversity curricula try to do) to
patch uncritically together such diverse and contradictory
multicultural pieces into something that the public relations
jargon identifies as "the beautiful quilt" or "a glorious
mosaic," become ultimately an exercise in futility if not
counter-productivity. News from many diversity-professing
schools is of increased student intolerance, campus conflicts,
instances of bigotry and of ethnic, racial, and cultural
separatism. What one could do, armed by the spirit of liberal
education with the time-tested elements of liberal wisdom, is
to search critically for those values common in _certain_
cultures, evaluate them as to their trans-cultural
appropriateness and labor to forge out of such compatible
elements common bonds which could last us into the future.
But this is, unfortunately, not what the various accrediting
bureaucracies are striving to enforce.

There is still another aspect to the "diversity" issue that this
essay has not addressed because it lacks intrinsic merit. "In
the minds of some," the Commission statement says,
"'diversity' has come to invoke 'political correctness,' an
intimidating environment on campus that discourages
individuals from freely expressing themselves within the
very broad boundaries set by judicial interpretation of the
First Amendment of the Bill of Rights." The Commission
wisely dissociates "diversity" from the notorious policy
known as "political correctness." This policy rejects the
"pursuit of truth," which has been the traditional goal of
higher education, for a thought-control aimed at the
modification of attitudes and feelings in our society, even at
the cost of academic freedom and truth itself.

Within the ideology of "political correctness," non-Westerners
are presumed to be the victims of Westerners and Western
culture. To rectify this supposed injustice and imbalance, a
systematic bias is used to penalize and depress all things
tainted with the West in any way that is hypothesized to be
prejudicial to non-Westerners. Consequently, in this
perspective, even the accidental relationship of classical
education to Athens and Rome is enough to render classical
education offensive to non-Europeans and as such to be
rightly censured and manipulated to help bring out a new
social order.

The sophistry of this argument is too patent to need
extended comment. For readers of good sense, it is enough to
note a simple little comment that St. Thomas Aquinas makes
to a student brother who has asked for advice on how best to
acquire wisdom. Among a small number of well chosen
remarks, St. Thomas advises the student that in human
wisdom it is paramount to note what is said and not who said
it. This is a plain truth obvious to any who would but reflect
on it sincerely. How monstrous it would be to reverse the
priorities and say "consider chiefly, or only, who said it, and
not the merit of what was said"!/2 The mind that would give
such counsel would in the name of some tortured ideology
violate its own very nature and will its own destruction. The
life spring of the intellect, its basic aspiration for truth,
would be suffocated at the source. This suicidal counsel
would change intellectual life into intellectual death.

Footnotes

/1 Although quantum theory of physics involves observers as
triggers of the act of observation, such observers are still a
part of the objective theoretical framework and have no
reactive influence on the process at hand.

/2 And thus be in lockstep with Nazi physicist Philipp Lenard,
who wrote four volumes of _German_Physics_ as distinct
from Einsteinian "Jewish" physics, or with that member of the
Soviet Politburo, A. A. Zhdanov, who urged his fellow
committee comrades, on behalf of the progressive
deterministic Marx-Engels science, to exterminate all
corrupting influences of the Plutocratic, bourgeois,
oppressive, probabilistic physics, chemistry, biology, etc.

