REASSERT YOUR DIGNITY, BROWN!

Jeffrey M. Duban

>From the time that Brown female undergraduates first made
headlines by scrawling the names of fellow-student "rapists"
on bathroom walls, Brown has shown leader-of-the-pack
determination in matters of political correctness. Though the
University of Pennsylvania has run a close second, with
Antioch College seeking to close the gap, Brown's regrettable
prominence in _Newsweek's_ "Sexual Correctness" cover
story of last Fall demonstrates that Brown is to be neither
outrun nor outdone.

The zeal of Brown's speech-code enforcement, of its
judiciary "tribunals," of its "date-rape" counselings and "play
actings" makes the head reel and gut churn. The
McCarthyesque or, these days, MacKinnonesque, atmosphere
of accusation, the date-rape and harassment hysteria (a
loosened necktie spells personal ruin) and the fostering of a
pervasive victim mentality (all women the victims of all men,
all men the victims of their own libidos, all minorities the
victims of white males) are the stuff of paranoids and
emotional basket cases. Intellectotomy is the treatment of
choice at Brown.

If AIDS is the problem, as Associate Dean of Student Life
Toby Simon urges in the September/October issue of
_Change_, then AIDS should be addressed consistently with
Dean Simon's desire for "a responsible, non-hysterical, non-
alarmist response by the university community." The
predicate for such response is not "mandatory sexual-assault
education," but a _college_education_. Nonconforming
feminist scholar Camille Paglia should be required reading
for all incoming freshmen, preceding and during orientation
week.

Today's politically and sexually correct Brown University is
not a place where, to paraphrase President Vartan
Gregorian's "Welcome" in the Dedication brochure, young
men and women may be shown how to attain greatness. It is
not a place of which famed Brown classicist John Rowe
Workman would be "proud and pleased," or which he would
consider to be "thriving."

**[On Oct. 9, 1993, Brown dedicated the John Rowe Workman
Distinguished Professorship in Classics and Humanistic
Tradition to Professor Workman, who died in 1985 after
having retired from a 38-year career teaching classics at the
university.]**

Free expression and inquiry are stifled by dogma and
orthodoxy. Sound education is subordinated to aberrant
forays into social transformation. Brown is plague-ridden,
like Thebes at the opening of _Oedipus_Rex_, the entrails
sputtering and overhung with sickly smoke.

Though the evidence is still sparse, _The_Wall_Street_Journal
recently opined that college campuses are slowly emerging
from the "multicultural" night. If _The Wall_Street_Journal_ is
any guide, the power is Dr. Gregorian's, as President of
Brown, to help redeem the legacy. Will he dare to decline the
challenge?

"The call, at any rate, is the university president's to make,"
said the editorial, "and it must be made quickly, without
shilly-shallying. Universities find themselves in their current
quagmires of notoriety precisely because their
administrators were more interested in evading politically
uncomfortable stands than in taking decisions that were
theirs to make. Instead of leading, they consulted with
endless committees and activists, placating each. That is
how the speech and harassment codes came into being in the
first place -- how governance of universities came under the
control of undergraduates and faculty ideologues."

Similarly, Christopher Lasch (_The_Culture_of_Narcissism_,
1979) attributed the progressive dissolution of higher
education to (among other things) "... programs in black
studies, women's studies, and other forms of consciousness
raising _for_no_other_purpose_than_to_head_off_political
_discontent_"  (emphasis added).

In a frenetic tarantella of capitulation (or is it _danse
_macabre_?), Brown cannot step quickly enough to embrace
the overwrought and barely post-adolescent sensibilities of
groups, whether in matters of comportment, curriculum,
admissions or hiring. Consider the nerve and utter self-
absorption of minority freshmen protesting their "under-
representation" on campus!

While any one of them may yet change the world, it is their
time and place, as freshmen, to be changed themselves. They
are privileged sojourners. If the university is not to their
liking, give them a tuition refund and send them home.

Students require and are entitled to an _education_. Seeking
to avoid the imposition  of discipline and hard work that
accompany this mandate, they would sooner remake the
university in their own mirror-shallow images. To quote
Erica Jong, writing in _The_New_Yorker_ on the "privilege of
spend[ing] college and graduate school immersed in the
classics": "Are African-Americans to be allowed to study only
their ancestors' myths and legends? Are women to be denied
Homer for a steady diet of Catherine MacKinnon? Can we be
so arrogant as to claim that contemporary polemic is all of
knowledge?"

The classical and humanistic learning for which Brown has
sought to commemorate Professor Workman is mocked -- as
is Professor Workman himself -- by the plague of political
correctness on campus. What will Brown's President do to
restore the integrity of Professor Workman's vision to the
professorship endowed in his name, to see that the
institution again "thrives."?

Jeffrey M. Duban, a member of Brown's class of 1971,
practices law in New York City. He formerly taught classics at
Georgia State University and Ohio State University.  His
comment appeared in a slightly modified form in the
November 5, 1993 issue of the Providence Journal Bulletin.

