<< Letter to the Editors >>

To the Editors:

Emerson may have had a point when he said that "a foolish
consistency is the hobgoblin of little minds," but a certain
degree of consistency is necessary if a person -- or a
publication -- is to remain intellectually coherent.  The
November/December issue of _MEASURE_ was a spectacular
example of the sort of cloudy confusion that hampers the
efforts of conservative and moderate liberals to fight
political correctness in our nation's schools.

You publish a very able defense of academic freedom at
Brigham Young University by Paul Kurtz, a defense which
quite correctly points out the arbitrary and dictatorial nature
of the Mormon Church's attempt to stifle dissent in that
school.  But then you choose to print a letter from a
Christian enthusiast who _praises_ religious indoctrination,
and who condemns Mary Lefkowitz of Wellesley for
questioning it in a previous article.  Perhaps you will answer
that _MEASURE_ is presenting a balance of viewpoints, but I
wonder about that.  In the same issue you include a piece by
E. Michael Jones, the general tenor of which is to champion
the right of the Catholic Church to police what happens at
Notre Dame University.  There is an unexamined idol of the
cave here, as Bacon might have said, and it's time that you
faced it and dealt with it.

As a matter of fact, I agree with E. Michael Jones on the
situation at Notre Dame, and with his analysis of the
shameless way in which a venal and temporizing Father
Hesburgh betrayed his church's trust for Ford Foundation
dollars and personal advancement.  I also agree that
ecclesiastical authorities have the right to control the nature
and scope of the theology, ethics, and dogmatics that are
taught in schools which they sponsor and support.  But this
is as far as such control should go.  There is no Catholic
biology, or Mormon literary criticism, or Baptist ancient
history, or Jewish physics.  Fields that have no doctrinal
content or implications should be utterly free from sectarian
meddling.  If they are not, the religious schools will suffer
from the same totalizing, smothering narrowness that
currently afflicts colleges where political correctness
dominates.

It is well to remember that the _doyen_ of the academic left,
Stanley Fish, has seized on exactly this point in his defense
of a blatantly politicized and partisan pedagogy.  If religious
schools can control the terms of internal debate in the name
of their chosen orthodoxies, says Fish, then why can't we in
the secular schools do the same?  An orthodoxy is an
orthodoxy, argues Fish, and if religionists can impose one in
a denominational institution, politically correct colleges
should be able to dictate one to their faculties as well.  It's
nice to hear Fish admitting that political correctness is a
religion, but it's not nice to see that too many of us who
oppose political correctness are making it possible for him
to construct such an argument.  Regardless of our personal
affection for religious schools, we should see to it that
ecclesiastical control over pedagogy at such colleges is
strictly limited in the manner I have described above.

Consistency, as I say, demands this of us, but strategic
prudence dictates it as well.  _Academic_freedom_is_much
_more_vitally_important_to_conservatives_and_moderates
_right_now_than_it_has_ever_been_before_.  Unless we are
consciously committed to its defense everywhere, including
denominational colleges, it will be safe nowhere.  The
religious right will crush dissent in the schools that they
control, and the secular left will crush it everywhere else.  I
believe Sidney Hook would have agreed with me.

In concrete terms, this means that we should work to foster
an atmosphere in higher education where no one dares to
tell any professor of a non-doctrinal subject in any school
what to think or how to teach or how to express himself
publicly and privately.  Granted, this will allow tenured
imposters like Leonard Jeffries and Barbara Ehrenreich and
their various clones around the nation to remain where they
are.  It will also allow the grossly incompetent faculty of
whom Mary Lefkowitz complains to remain in place.  And it
will force religious schools to put up with all sorts of
nonsense from less than orthodox faculty.  But more
crucially, it will permit the rest of us (who constitute, at this
point, a dwindling minority for common sense) to stay in
academia and keep fighting.

Joseph S. Salemi

New York University

