SENSITIVITY TRAINING:  A TWO-EDGED SWORD

Linda Seebach

Administrators at the University of California, Riverside,
have discovered that "sensitivity training" is a two-edged
sword.

Their attempt to punish Phi Kappa Sigma fraternity for a T-
shirt design that displeased Chicano activists on campus was
thwarted when the Individual Rights Foundation sued the
University, alleging it had violated members' Constitutional
rights.

As part of the settlement, IRF president John Howard
demanded that the administrators participate in "sensitivity
training" sessions on the First Amendment.

That provision was the one "non-negotiable demand" of the
settlement,  Howard said, and from now on it will be a matter
of policy when the IRF challenges university administrators.

"I was thinking about what I could do to get their attention,"
said Howard, who clearly relishes the idea.

IRF's activities are definitely getting attention.  It offers free
legal assistance to students and others who are trapped in
the Kafkaesque world created by campus thought police.

At Occidental College in Los Angeles, it won an agreement
from the administration to rewrite its sexual harassment
code to be consistent with the First Amendment.
Occidental's harassment code was even broader than such
policies commonly are, providing that people could be
punished for speech that offended anyone who happened to
overhear it.

The Occidental law suit was brought on behalf of members
of Alpha Tau Omega fraternity.

Another California case, at California State University,
Northridge, involved a chapter of Zeta Beta Tau, which was
punished for a party invitation that referred to an offensive
song.  CSUN officials ended the fraternity's 14-month
suspension after the IRF entered the case.

The IRF's efforts in California have been aided by a law that
came into effect just this year.  It guarantees students the
same First Amendment rights on campus that they would
have off campus, and provides that the students can collect
court costs and attorney's fees if their rights are violated.

The non-profit group is a spin-off of the Center for the Study
of Popular Culture started by David Horowitz and Peter
Collier.  The Center also publishes the anti-PC academic
tabloid _Heterodoxy_.

Howard's clients so far have been mostly fraternity
members, because fraternities have been the favorite targets
of PC administrators.  In any cases, the fraternities' national
officers have hastened to punish their own chapters.

"They think if they fall on their own sword they can avoid
sanctions" by the university, Howard said.  "That isn't
supportive of their members, and it's wrong ... it just
encourages them."

Howard welcomes inquiries from students and others whose
free-speech rights have been infringed by campus officials.
The IRF can be reached at 800-752-6562.

Linda Seebach is a writer for the _Los_Angeles_Daily_News_

