>}>third mod 7 is illegal if PKPs patent is worth the paper it is written
>}>on, but that's a different point.
>}
>}How did you ever come to this ridiculous conclusion?
>
>A while back, a year or two ago, someone posted a notice about a
>program they had which used a form of public key encryption other than
>PKP.  I believe it may even have been cross-posted to
>alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk.  He got a letter from PKP, which he posted
>here, ordering him to stop distributing his program and listing the
>things (aside from RSA itself) which PKP has patented.  One of them
>was exponentiation on a finite field.

You are either mistaken in your understanding of what they claimed, or
they made a provably false claim.  There are certainly many variations
on the basic RSA algorithm that are covered by the RSA patent--however,
it is simply not possible that exponentionation in a finite field (generally)
is one of them.  There are, undoubtedly, other important qualifiers.

The folks who created RSA had no claim for a patent on all uses of modular
exponentiation.  Modular exponentiation was well-know long before Rivest,
Shamir and Adleman were even born.  If I recall the chronology correctly,
RSA wasn't even the first use of modular exponentiation as a cryptosystem.
modular exponentiation with a prime modulus had been previously used as
a secret-key cryptosystem.

(To avoid any meaningless argument, I'll just say that if, by some monumental
screw-up by the USPTO, PKP had been granted a patent on modular exponentiation,
I would bet my *life* that any infringement claim based on that alone (e.g.,
taking 2^3 mod 7) would be thrown out in a summary judgement of the defense.
There can be no argument as to the facts--that modular exponentiation
has been around for millenia.  The only issue is a simple matter of law--PKP
can't possibly be eligible to hold a patent on it.)


