Is it your position that anything which "hurts" people, or which carries
the potential to "hurt" people, should or may be outlawed?

If so, then your proposed ban should logically extend to anti-war
speech.  After all (to cite just a few examples), it weakens a nation's
resolve to go to war or continue a war, it increases the likelihood that
the soldiers already in the field will receive less support and
reinforcements -- probably resulting in the deaths of some of them -- it
increases the likelihood that the civilians, domestic or foreign who
would benefit from a continuation of the war effort will suffer more
than they would have if the anti-war speech had been suppressed, and, on
the homefront, it probably raises the blood pressure and otherwise
upsets and "hurts" those people who support the war.

My point is that virtually _all_ speech either "hurts" or carries the
potential of "hurting" _somebody_.  If that is your criteria for
censorship, you'll have to support the censorship of just about
everything (including, of course, calls for censorship).

